@h1 Choose your path h1@

You've seen the shape of Wit — prose by default, structure when you reach for it,
five layers you climb only as far as a document needs. What comes next depends on
why you're here. There are two doors, and picking wrong costs nothing: the tracks
converge, and you can cross over any time.

@h2 I want to write h2@

You keep a manuscript — a report, an essay, a thesis, a set of docs — in git as
plain text, and compile a reader-ready draft in seconds. Your day is prose, with
structure where the argument needs it: a heading, a figure, a citation, a table
built from your data, a section that appears only in the internal draft.

A taste of where this goes — your own reusable chapter component:

@@@pre
#chapter ||number, title||
@h2 ::number:: — ::title:: h2@
...
chapter#

@chapter |number I| |title The Keeper|
For eleven days the lamp had burned untended.
chapter@
pre@@@

The @strong Write track strong@ starts with the fundamentals —
@a |href /docs/write/prose/| paragraphs and prose a@,
@a |href /docs/write/emphasis/| emphasis a@,
@a |href /docs/write/comments/| comments a@,
@a |href /docs/write/headings-structure/| headings and structure a@, and
@a |href /docs/write/lists/| lists a@ — then builds up to components, citations,
multi-file manuscripts, and the draft-in-git workflow that ties it all together.

@h2 I want to build h2@

You embed Wit in your own program — parsing it, walking its typed tree, loading
external data, rendering it your own way. Wit gives you a clean pipeline to hang
tools on: @code parse code@ turns source into a tree, @code resolve code@ binds
references, @code expand code@ runs the definitions and logic, and a renderer
turns the result into whatever you need.

A taste of where this goes — the whole pipeline in five lines:

@@@pre
import { parse } from '@witlang/parser';
import { resolve, expand } from '@witlang/runtime';
import { renderHtml } from '@witlang/render-html';

const doc = parse(source, 'report.wit');
const html = renderHtml(expand(resolve(doc)));
pre@@@

The @strong Build track strong@ walks the architecture, the AST, the resolve and
expand stages, the data-loading seam, and how to write a custom renderer or add a
node. Everything is typed, deterministic, and zero-dependency, so it drops into an
existing toolchain without dragging a runtime behind it. Start from
@a |href /docs/start/install/| the package install a@ and the built-in vocabulary
in the @a |href /docs/reference/core-vocabulary/| reference a@.

@h2 Where the paths meet h2@

Both doors lead to the same shared rooms. The
@a |href /docs/reference/core-vocabulary/| reference a@ lists every built-in node
and the element it produces. @a |href /docs/start/troubleshooting/| Troubleshooting a@
turns any red error into a lesson. And if you're arriving from another tool,
@a |href /docs/start/migration-markdown/| Migration from Markdown a@ and
@a |href /docs/start/migration-latex/| Migration from LaTeX a@ convert your
existing fluency directly. The in-browser playground lets you edit Wit and watch
it render live, no install required.

@h2 Not sure? h2@

Default to writing. It's the larger of the two audiences and the faster payoff,
and nothing you learn there is wasted if you later want to build on Wit — the
mental model is the same either way. Pick the door that fits today; the other one
stays open.

@h2 See also h2@

If you skipped ahead, @a |href /docs/start/what-is-wit/| What is Wit? a@ sets the
scene and @a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| the mental model a@ names the
pattern. For a full guided build, follow
@a |href /docs/start/tutorial/| the tutorial a@.
